  "grammar_notes": [
    "-re is a suffix meaning 'he is' or 'there are'.",
    "mo means 'good' because -re is 'he is', so more = 'he is good'.",
    "ru-/roo- is a prefix indicating 'made by hand' or manufactured items.",
    "-ne is a questioning mode marker suffix.",
    "pa means 'in the'.",
    "pe means 'step'.",
    "sacke means 'on top of'.",
    "soppe means 'on top'.",
    "mau means 'place/place of' and also 'there'.",
    "to means 'little' and can be suffixed to form diminutives (e.g. he-to = little man, yi-to = little woman, seunne-to = little river/creek).",
    "Ya- is a prefix meaning 'person-genuine'.",
    "ya-/yi- are second person possessive prefixes meaning 'yours/your'.",
    "wa is a third person possessive pronoun meaning 'his/hers/its/theirs'.",
    "hore means 'he is a'.",
    "Eraute is cognated in proto-Siouan and the e- is probably a second person conjugational affix for the verb as a whole.",
    "Eraute may actually mean 'shall I give it to you to eat?'.",
    "raute means 'chew'; ra is 'mouth' as an instrumental prefix.",
    "Compound words are formed by combining existing roots (e.g. yupwaure = yup 'wood' + waure 'spear' = 'Spear Tree' = loblolly).",
    "yupyupseunne = yup+yup+seunne = 'tree lined river' (runlet).",
    "Numbers above ten are formed by combining soone (more than/ten) with smaller numbers (e.g. soone nomme = twelve).",
    "hauk functions as 'plus' in number constructions (e.g. tonne hauk sea = eleven).",
    "Could 'Roa' = leader? More = good or excel at, Roamore = king.",
    "Morene = More (good) + -ne (questioning mode marker) = 'Are you good?'",
    "pepere may mean 'there are steps' (pe + pe + -re)."
  ],
  "pronunciation_notes": [
    "Capital letters in pronunciation = longer/emphasis.",
    "au = a-oo.",
    "The r in 'hor' (blowing) is trilled slightly, as in Spanish, but less trilly.",
    "Woccon consonant inventory: P, t, k, ?, č, s, h, m, n, r, w, y.",
    "Woccon shows long oral vowels.",
    "The ą' in wą'se (arrow/awl) needs further examination.",
    "yauh (path) may alternatively be spelled 'yaub' according to comparative dictionary."
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    "Woccon is a Siouan language.",
    "Woccon village was located at Wheat Field near the juncture of the Neuse River and Contentnea Creek west of the Pamlico Sound.",
    "Source references include Rudes (2000), Rudes (2004), Rountree/Taukchiray (2021), Carter (p. 173), Koontz (Siouan syncopating r-stems), Adelung & Vater (p. 178), Rudin (p. 21), csd.clld.org (comparative Siouan dictionary), and a living dictionary.",
    "Catawba cognate for 'powerful': wakąd (p. 9?).",
    "In all but Crow (aho), Tutelo, and Catawba (Hawuh), 'thank you' = hahó in Siouan languages.",
    "Ball Knock (wap-ka-hare) is a traditional game.",
    "Highlighted in blue = changed on 12/21/2025. Highlighted yellow = where pronunciation work stopped last.",
    "Koontz source URL: https://lx.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/koontz_-_siouan_syncopating_r-stems.pdf"
  ]
}